.Dd November 13, 2026
.Dt LUMTAIL-RULES 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm lumtail-rules
.Nd custom log parsing rules and text templates for lumtail
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Read this page to write a JSON rules file for an application's text log and
choose how its records appear in
.Xr lumtail 1 .
Load a file explicitly:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --rules application-rules.json application.log
.Ed
.Pp
Rules files add named modules to the built-in parsers.
Each module has a match pattern, optional severity aliases and a text
display template.
Steps derive more fields from a match; see
.Sx STEPS .
.Sh FILE FORMAT
A rules file is a JSON object with exactly these properties:
.Bl -tag -width modules
.It Cm version
Required integer, 1.
.It Cm modules
Required array of module objects.
An empty array is accepted.
.El
.Pp
Use JSON strings and escapes for patterns and templates.
Unknown properties and duplicate JSON object keys are errors, including keys
with equivalent escaped spellings.
Each file is validated before any of its modules are added.
.Sh MODULE PROPERTIES
.Bl -tag -width priority
.It Cm name
Required string, unique across built-in and loaded modules.
Use 1 through 64 ASCII letters, digits, underscores or hyphens.
Names are case-sensitive.
.It Cm pattern
Required string containing literal text and named brace captures, or an array
of up to 32 such strings.
The first alternative that matches the whole header line wins.
Each alternative contains at least one capture.
See
.Sx MATCH PATTERNS .
.It Cm priority
Integer from -999 through 1000; the default is 100.
Higher priorities are tried first for each record.
Equal priorities keep registration order: built-ins first, then files in
command-line order and modules in array order.
The first complete match wins.
.It Cm levels
Optional object mapping nonempty input labels to severity strings.
Alias keys are case-insensitive and must be distinct after case folding.
Each value names TRACE, DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR or CRITICAL, or one of their
aliases in
.Xr lumtail 1 .
.It Cm display
Optional object with
.Cm template
and
.Cm color
properties, described below.
.It Cm set
Object mapping a field to a template of literal text and
.Ql {field}
references, applied after the pattern.
A template with a missing reference leaves its field unchanged.
.It Cm map
Object mapping a field to an object of exact value replacements.
.It Cm omit
Array of values; an emitted field, including
.Cm level ,
that holds one of them is dropped.
.It Cm steps
Array of up to 64 step objects, applied in order; see
.Sx STEPS .
.El
.Pp
The built-in names
.Cm json , cef , leef , fail2ban , bind.query , unbound.log , cri.log , syslog ,
.Cm web-error , access , haproxy.http , ids.fast ,
.Cm aws.vpc-flow , squid.native , glog.text , osquery.status , fortigate.kv ,
.Cm keycloak.log , freeradius.log , dirsrv.access , auditd.raw , logfmt.kv
and
.Cm plain
are reserved.
Use
.Ql lumtail --rules application-rules.json --list-modules
to inspect the resulting order.
Default-priority custom modules run before the built-ins.
.Sh MATCH PATTERNS
A capture is written as
.Ql {field} .
Capture names follow
.Li [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]* .
Names are case-sensitive.
.Cm raw , format , file
and
.Cm line
are reserved for metadata.
.Pp
Literal text must match exactly, except that a run of spaces or tabs matches
one or more spaces or tabs.
By default, a capture ends at the first occurrence of the next literal
delimiter.
A final unqualified capture takes the remaining input.
Unqualified captures may be empty.
Use
.Ql {{
and
.Ql }}
for literal braces.
Adjacent captures, unmatched braces and invalid names are configuration errors.
Capture names must be unique within each pattern.
The discard capture
.Ql {_}
may repeat.
.Pp
.Ql {name:spec}
can restrict the captured value or change where it ends.
Use one value check per capture:
.Cm int
takes one or more ASCII digits,
.Cm ip
an IPv4 or IPv6 literal,
.Cm word
nonempty text without spaces or tabs, and
.Ql a|b
one of the listed values.
.Pp
.Cm words=N
consumes exactly N words separated by spaces or tabs, with N from 1 through 9.
.Cm last
splits at the last occurrence of the following literal, once per pattern.
Use either
.Cm words=N
or
.Cm last
to change where a capture ends.
Combine specs with commas, as in
.Ql {src_ip:ip,last} .
Names starting with
.Ql _
are working fields available to rule steps.
.Pp
For this pattern:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{timestamp} [{level}] {source}: {message}
.Ed
.Pp
and this input:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
2026-11-10T02:03:04Z [FAIL] billing: Payment rejected
.Ed
.Pp
the captures are:
.Bl -column timestamp "2026-11-10T02:03:04Z" -offset indent
.It Sy Field Ta Sy Value
.It timestamp Ta 2026-11-10T02:03:04Z
.It level Ta FAIL
.It source Ta billing
.It message Ta Payment rejected
.El
.Pp
With multiline assembly enabled, only the first physical line is parsed;
continuations stay attached to the logical record.
The normalized
.Cm level
uses the module's aliases, then the standard severity vocabulary.
The captured label stays in
.Cm original_level .
Without a recognized level the severity is UNKNOWN.
If the pattern omits
.Cm message ,
the input header supplies the message.
.Sh STEPS
A step accepts:
.Bl -tag -width min_level
.It Cm from
Required source field for a step with
.Cm pattern
or
.Cm kv .
.It Cm when , unless
Objects mapping a field to a value or an array of values.
The step runs only when every
.Cm when
field holds a listed value and no
.Cm unless
field does.
.It Cm pattern
String or array of alternatives matched against
.Cm from ;
captures overwrite fields.
.It Cm kv
Nonempty separator for
.Ql key=value
pairs read from
.Cm from .
The separator can contain any text except an equals sign or a double quote.
Use a separate step for
.Cm pattern .
Double-quoted values lose their quotes and the backslash of
.Ql \e\(dq
and
.Ql \e\e .
Keys without a field of the module are emitted as they are.
.It Cm set , map
As on the module.
.It Cm level , min_level
Severity that replaces the level, or that the level is raised to.
.It Cm require
Boolean, false by default.
When true and the step's conditions hold, a failed pattern or
.Cm kv
split makes this module decline the record.
.El
.Pp
A step without
.Cm require
is skipped when its pattern fails.
This module reads
.Ql 2026-11-07 18:30:01,456 fail2ban.actions   [500]: NOTICE  [sshd] Ban 192.0.2.10 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
  "version": 1,
  "modules": [{
    "name": "fail2ban-rules",
    "pattern": "{_date} {_time} {source} [{pid:int}]: {level} {message}",
    "set": {"timestamp": "{_date} {_time}"},
    "steps": [
      {"from": "message",
       "pattern": "[{jail:word}] {action:Ban|Unban} {src_ip:ip}",
       "map": {"action": {"Ban": "ban", "Unban": "unban"}}},
      {"when": {"action": "ban"}, "min_level": "error"}
    ]
  }]
}
.Ed
.Sh DISPLAY TEMPLATES
The
.Cm display
object accepts:
.Bl -tag -width template
.It Cm template
String containing literal text and brace field references.
The default is
.Ql {timestamp} \[u2502] {level} \[u2502] {source} \[u2502] {message} .
.It Cm color
Either
.Cm level
(the default) or
.Cm none .
The former permits severity coloring when command-line color selection enables
it; the latter disables the module's severity coloring.
.El
.Pp
References start with an ASCII letter or underscore; subsequent characters
are ASCII letters, digits,
.Ql _ ,
.Ql . ,
.Ql @ ,
.Ql %
or
.Ql - .
This permits namespaced built-in fields such as
.Ql {syslog_sd.auth@32473.user} .
Use doubled braces to print literal braces.
.Pp
Templates can reference captured or normalized fields and these metadata:
.Bl -tag -width original_level
.It Cm raw
Original normalized record content.
.It Cm format
Selected module name.
.It Cm file
Input filename, or
.Ql -
for standard input.
.It Cm line
First physical line number, starting at 1.
.It Cm level
Normalized severity label.
.It Cm original_level
Producer label when available.
.El
.Pp
Missing fields display
.Ql - ;
present empty fields display an empty string.
The renderer escapes terminal controls and keeps ordinary Unicode.
Only the first
.Cm level
reference receives severity coloring.
.Pp
The command-line
.Fl -template
option uses the same grammar and overrides
.Fl -preset
regardless of argument order.
A preset overrides the module template.
Display settings affect text output; fields remain available to filters and
JSON export.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Save the following as
.Pa application-rules.json :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
  "version": 1,
  "modules": [
    {
      "name": "application",
      "priority": 100,
      "pattern": "{timestamp} [{level}] {source}: {message}",
      "levels": {"FAIL": "error", "PANIC": "critical"},
      "display": {
        "template": "{timestamp} [{level}] {source}: {message}",
        "color": "level"
      }
    }
  ]
}
.Ed
.Pp
Parse a record with the new module:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
echo '2026-11-10T02:03:04Z [FAIL] billing: Payment rejected' |
  lumtail --rules application-rules.json --color never
.Ed
.Pp
The text output is:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
2026-11-10T02:03:04Z [ERROR] billing: Payment rejected
.Ed
.Pp
Export its captured fields or select only this parser:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --rules application-rules.json --output json application.log
lumtail --rules application-rules.json --module application \e
  application.log
.Ed
.Pp
Load a second file and print each selected record's source location:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --rules application-rules.json --rules worker-rules.json \e
  --template '{file}:{line} [{level}] {message}' application.log
.Ed
.Pp
Use a lower priority when a custom pattern should run after a built-in parser;
for example, priority 50 follows the built-in access parser at priority 60.
The audit RAW parser and plain fallback have priority -1000, below every
permitted custom priority.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
An unreadable, invalid or oversized rules file causes a diagnostic on standard
error and exit status 2.
Unknown module names, reserved names and duplicate names also return 2.
Correct the named file before retrying.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr lumtail 1 ,
.Xr lumtail-agent 7 ,
.Xr lumtail-formats 7
.Sh LIMITS
A rules file is limited to 1048576 bytes and 64 JSON nesting levels.
The registry accepts up to 120 custom modules across all files, in addition
to the built-ins listed in
.Sx MODULE PROPERTIES .
Each pattern or template source is limited to 65536 bytes and 1024 tokens.
Expanded templates are limited to 16777216 bytes per record before terminal
escaping; exceeding that rendering limit returns status 1.
The same limit applies to the combined bytes of names and values emitted by a
custom rule, including values borrowed from the input.
.Pp
Patterns try each alternative once without backtracking.
A module accepts 64 steps, 32 alternatives per pattern, 64 values per choice and
1024 pairs per
.Cm kv
step.
Nested or escaped formats such as JSON and CEF need a built-in parser.
